# FORMAT — Code Academy, Document Standards & Scripting Specification
Floor: 2
Thrust: 9
Patch: 3
File: openOODA/FORMAT.oot

This document defines formatting standards for code academy headers, process documents, and test harnesses.
All documentation and code comments follow ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English.

## 0. Repository File Laws

The repository enforces strict file rules:
1. `.oo` ONLY — All product code, compiler passes, standard libraries, CLI tools, and test scripts.
2. `.oot` ONLY — All documentation, process boards, RFCs, and specifications.

Prohibited file extensions:
- No `.py` — Python is unauthorized across all workspace directories.
- No `.sh` — Except `bootstrap/oodac_pure_build` and `install.sh`.
- No `.oos` — Scripts use the standard `.oo` file extension.
- No `.md` — All documentation must use `.oot` files.

Line Limit: Every `.oo` and `.oot` file must contain a maximum of 256 lines. Comments count toward this limit.
Generated `.oot` files must not exceed 256 lines and must display the text: THIS FILE IS GENERATED — DO NOT EDIT.
Strict line limits maintain software quality. Large files increase software defects.

## 1. Part 1: .oo Code Academy Format

Every product `.oo` file begins with a standardized 4-element Academy header.

### 1.1 Header Structure

// # <Title>
//
// Logline: <One sentence in present tense, active voice, ending with a period.>
//
// Setup: <Preconditions, required capability tokens, or runtime assumptions.>
//
// Beats:
//   1. <First action or responsibility>
//   2. <Second action or responsibility>
//   3. <Third action or responsibility>
//
// residual: <Optional: features planned but not yet built>

### 1.2 ASD-STE100 Header Rules
- Title: Identifies the technical responsibility of the file.
- Logline: Exactly one descriptive sentence in ASD-STE100. Use present tense and active voice. End with a period.
- Setup: Declares preconditions, required capability tokens, and environment assumptions.
- Beats: Numbered list of 3 to 6 logical beats describing operations in sequence. Do not reference line numbers.
- Residual: Optional single line immediately following Beats for unbuilt features.

### 1.3 Code Readability Rules
- Write clear code for both compiler execution and human maintenance.
- The Academy header summarizes module responsibilities for human maintainers.
- Choose function and variable names that reveal their exact purpose.
- Retain standard function naming in compiler modules (e.g. `typecheck_*`, `c_emit_*`, `lex_*`).
- Do not use script-level control constructs in compiler passes.
- Write expressive code to minimize the need for inline comments.
- Obey the 256-line limit. Split large files along clear domain interfaces.

### 1.4 Code Quality Loop
- Run `./oodac/oodac check <file.oo>` after making modifications.
- If the check passes, proceed to the next module.
- If the check fails, repair the error immediately before proceeding.

## 2. Part 2: .oot Document Format

All process boards, RFCs, documentation, and technical notes use the `.oot` format.

### 2.1 Document Header Structure
Every `.oot` file begins with a standardized 4-element header block:

# <NAME> — <Domain or Purpose Title>
Floor: 2
Thrust: 9
Patch: 3
File: openOODA/<NAME>.oot

### 2.2 Section Structure & Formatting
- Title: H1 heading with board name and technical domain.
- Numbered Headings: Use `## 1.` and `### 1.1` to maintain visual hierarchy.
- ASD-STE100 Compliance: Required for all process boards, RFCs, and handoff reports.
  Write short sentences. Put one thought in each sentence. Use direct technical words.
  Use active voice. Do not use slang, jokes, or metaphors.
- Status Markers: Use standardized status vocabulary:
  - `done` — Verified on disk with proof.
  - `smoke` — Small verification check passes.
  - `partial` — Partially implemented and compiling.
  - `residual` — Planned, but not yet built.
  - `open` — In progress during the active sprint.
- Line Limit: All `.oot` files must contain a maximum of 256 lines.

### 2.3 Generational Semantic Versioning Specification
All process boards, package manifests, and release tags follow Generational Semantic Versioning:
- Format: `v<Floor>.<Thrust>.<Patch>` (Verified Ecosystem Release State: `v2.9.3` from `ooda.pkg`).
- Floor (F): Generational foundation tier substrate (Gen 1-4). Increments on breaking changes to core memory or capability ABI.
- Thrust (T): Boyd E-M performance improvements, compiler optimization passes, and non-breaking feature additions.
- Patch (P): Adversarial security remediation, hostile test certifications, and bug repairs.
- Power Law 80/20 Floor Rule: Increment Floor only when the 20% core foundational primitives change.

## 3. Part 3: Test and Tool Script Standards

### 3.1 Naming and Locations
- Smoke tests and proof scripts live in `ooda/scripts/` or `qa/`.
- Name test scripts with descriptive suffixes: `*_smoke.oo`, `*_proof.oo`, `*_test.oo`.

### 3.2 Script Structure
- All scripts must contain the 4-element Academy header.
- Scripts must handle capabilities explicitly through `pub fn main(...)`.
- Failed assertions must terminate execution with non-zero exit codes.

### 3.3 Verification Execution Commands
- Type check: `./bin/ooda check <file.oo>`
- Execute program: `./bin/ooda run <file.oo>`
- Run master governance check: `./bin/ooda run openOODA/scripts/enforcer.oo`
- Check process board hygiene: `./bin/ooda run openOODA/scripts/check_board.oo`

## 4. Writing Style Quick Reference

Follow these five rules for all documentation:
1. Max 20 words per sentence for instructions and rules.
2. Max 25 words per sentence for descriptions.
3. Put exactly one thought in each sentence.
4. Use active voice verbs (e.g. "The compiler emits code", not "Code is emitted by the compiler").
5. State facts directly without rhetorical flourishes or marketing terms.

